  year = {1972},
  volume = {50},
  pages = {833--838},
  issn = {0008-4018},
  abstract = {The fatty acids of phospholipids and of triglycerides from the lings and heart of P.vitulina concolor show distinctive differences from the depot (blubber) fat. Primarily, this is due to greatly elevated levels of 20:4 omega 6 in the phospholipids. Although these have about the same total C sub(20) and C sub(22) polyethylenic acids with 5 and 6 double bonds as the depot fat, they occur in different proportions emphasizing 20:5 omega 3 rather than 22:6 omega 3. The organ triglycerides resemble the phospholipids in saturated fatty acid composition and the depot fats in monoethylenic fatty acid composition, but have remarkably low levels of 20:5 omega 3, 22:5W3, and 22.66 omega 3 compared to the depot fat. Comparisons are made with phospholipids from the lungs of the leatherback turtle Dermochelys coriacea coriacea Linnaeus but the novel trans-6-hexadecenoic acid found in the turtle lipids could not be detected in the seal lipids.},
