  year = {1974},
  volume = {31},
  pages = {333--341},
  abstract = {The fatty acids of triglycerides in the hearts from 2 harbour seals (P. vitulina) and 1 grey seal (H. grypus) differed quantitatively in some details from those in the blubber. The 22:1 found in the heart triglycerides was approx 1/2 that in the blubber triglycerides. Isomer distributions differed within 20:1 acids in the 2 triglycerides for both spp, the higher proportion of 20:1 omega 11 fatty acid deposited in the heart than in the blubber indicating more active chain shortening from 22:1. The fatty acids of phospholipids of all 3 hearts and livers also differed quantitatively in some fatty acid details indicating organ-specific compositions. These also differed from compositions of similar lipids in terrestrial animals in their generally marine fatty acid composition which were established for the blubbers. The grey seal (age 3 months) showed unusual percentages of linoleic types of acids in the organ lipids and also a curious enrichment in omega 7 monoethylenic acids. Dietary factors are believed to be responsible for differences between this animal and the 2 harbour seals (aged 10 and 23 months).},
