  doi = {10.1139/f84-100},
  abstract = {The DDE/SIGMA DDT (SIGMA DDT = DDT + DDE + DDD) ratios in the blubber of North Atlantic pinnipeds and odontocete cetaceans are compiled to obtain a biannual mean series. Conversion of DDT to DDE is interpreted as being due not only to the marine mammals but also to the overall detoxifying activity of the North Atlantic marine biota. The change of these ratios during 1964-81 is examined; a strong correlation between these ratios and time is found in both groups, ie this index can be tentatively used when assessing the chronology of pollutant input into the ecosystem. An equilibrium value may be reached at the beginning of the next century when the ratio reaches a value of c0.60, provided that no new inputs of contaminant into North Atlantic waters exist. -from Author},
