  abstract = {Samples of liver. muscle, and kidney from fur seal, Cal/orilillll., IIrsillllS, and liver from harbor seal, PiIonl "illililla ric/wr"i, were analyzed for total mercury. Liver and kidney of fur seals were analyzed for lead, cadmium, and arsenic Fur seals were from the Pribilof Islands, Alaska. and from off the Washington coast. Harbor seals wcre from the waters of southern California, Oregon, Washington, and the Bering Sea. All of the samples, including a fetus taken 3 mo before birth. contained mercury. lead, and cadmium. Arsenic was not detected. Mercury was highest in liver. followed by kidney, then muscle. The maximum concentration of mercury in liver was about 700 ppm in a southern Calillnnia harbor seal and 170 ppm in a fur seal taken oil the Washington coast. Concentrations of cadmium and lead were highest in the kidney (maximums of LX ppm lead and 15.6 ppm cadmium) of fur seals. Concentrations of mercury did not correlate with age in muscle or kidney (P\textquestiondown{} 0.05) but did correlate significantly with age in liver (P \textexclamdown{} O.O). Concentrations of cadmium and lead in liver and kidney did not correlate with age (/' \textquestiondown{} 0.(5). In samples of liver collected from harbor seals, the highest concentrations of mercury were from southern California seals.},
