  abstract = {Recent studies have shown that the complementary analysis of mercury(Hg) concentrations and stable isotopic ratios of nitrogen (delta(15)N)carbon (delta(13)C) can be useful for investigating the trophicon the Hg exposure and accumulation in marine top predators.this study, we propose to evaluate the interspecies variability of Hgin phocids from polar areas and to compare Hgbetween both hemispheres. Mercury concentrations,(15)N and delta(13)C were measured in fur from 85 individuals7 phocidae species, a Ross seal (Ommatophoca rossii),seals (Leptonychotes weddellii), crabeater seals (Lobodon), harbour seals (Phoca vitulina), grey seals (Halichoerus), ringed seals (Pusa hispida) and a bearded seal (Erignathus), from Greenland, Denmark and Antarctica. Our results showed acorrelation between Hg concentrations and delta(15)N valuesall individuals. Seals from the Northern ecosystems displayedHg concentrations, delta(15)N and delta(13)C values than thosethe Southern waters. Those geographical differences in Hg andisotopes values were likely due to higher environmental Hgand somewhat greater number of steps in Arctic food webs., dissimilarities in feeding habits among species were showndelta(15)N and delta(13)C analysis, resulting in an importantvariation in fur Hg concentrations. A trophic segregationobserved between crabeater seals and the other species, resultingthe very specific diet of krill of this species and leading to theobserved Hg concentrations.},
