  abstract = {The review by Sam Ridgway of the monograph, Diving and Asphyxia (MARINE MAMMAL SCIENCE 2(1):82-83), raises a question over which marine mammal physiologists have puzzled for some years. It concerns the wide range of responses to dives, natural and experimental, their designations as representing one or another categories of 'diving dogma' depending upon whether the dives are forced, trained or natural. The distinctions are more apparent than real. The literature indicates that diving responses are highly variable and defy easy categorization. The variations observed are graded responses depending upon many conditions, including whether or not the animal is frightened, relaxed, exercising, restrained, free or drugged.},
