  volume = {18},
  pages = {1--34},
  publisher = {{American Geophysical Union}},
  address = {{Washington, D.C.}},
  abstract = {includes a discussion of all the Arctocephalinae, with details on cranial measurements useful (or not) in distinguishing the 9 species of fur seals. the authors question the validity of separating the Otariidae into two sub-families, pointing out that numerous researchers have mis-identified sea lion skulls as fur seals. In separating Callorhinus and Arctocephalus, Repenning et al., found that Callorhinus generally has a shorter rostrum; more inflated maxillae anterior to the orbit; smaller teeth; more ornamented medial margins of the ectotympanic bones, such that a concave bulla is frequently produced; and more elongated flippers than Arctocephalus. The only other useful skeletal character they mention is that the facial angle (the angle formed by a) the line across the occipital crest (but not on the sagital crest, if elevated) and along the highest portion of the supraorbital region and b) touching the premaxillary tuberosity and the tip of the nasal bones near the midline. However, they point out that this angle is affected by growth of the skull and may not work in juveniles. Within Arctocephalus, skull length, tooth size and pattern, rostral length, height of forehead, and width of palate between the molars are the best for distinguishing species (see text for specific details).},
  keywords = {ID's,kakanui}
}

