  pages = {2047--2048},
  abstract = {Responses of marine mammals to human-generated sound are of interest due to concern for protection of animals with sensitive hearing from acoustic harassment or injury. Goold and Fish [J. Acoust. Sec. Am. 103, 2177-2184 (1998)] compare their limited observations of wild common dolphins (Delphinus delphis) near operating seismic vessels off Wales, with studies of trained bottlenose dolphins (Tursiops truncatus) in San Diego Bay [Ridgway et al., Tech. Rpt. 1751, NCCOSC RDTE (1997)]. Because of the considerable difference between numerous seismic pulses and the single 1-s tones employed by Ridgway et nl., the comparison is not valid. Further, Goold and Fish do not accurately represent the bottlenose dolphin responses. (C) 1999 Acoustical Society of America. [S0001-4966(99)03903-X]. [References: 7]},
